ONE PROBE, reverted. In the `case 2` arm, the three lines
  tp2 = (s32*)(e + offsetof(BlitEntry, timer));
  *tp2 += gFrameTicks;
  t = *tp2;
became
  *(s32*)(e + offsetof(BlitEntry, timer)) += gFrameTicks;
  tp2 = (s32*)(e + offsetof(BlitEntry, timer));
  t = *tp2;
i.e. the cursor is materialised AFTER the first dereference instead of before it, which is the order the target's own stream shows (`lwz r3,8(r19)` at +0x1a4 THEN `addi r25,r19,8` at +0x1a8; we emit `addi r25,r21,8` at +0x19c THEN `lwz r4,8(r21)`). The rationale was a same-session precedent: on show_optmenu this run, moving an assignment relative to its neighbours flipped a copy between the `mr` and `addi rD,rS,0` forms and made it byte-exact, which is a genuinely new source-level idea against the LZ law's 'not a source property' framing and is what justified reopening an axis the corpus calls allocator-determined.

RESULT: NEGATIVE, and instructive about arbitration. real 251 -> 243 (an 8-row gain) while the instruction counts held at T279/O280 AND the opcode multiset held at exactly 7t - and fresh full-ninja fuzzy fell 79.806% -> 78.104%, a 1.70-point drop on a 1116-byte function. Reverted; the tree re-measured back to real 251 / T279/O280 / 7t. THE ARBITRATION RULE THIS CONFIRMS: when `real` is the ONLY predictor that moves - counts flat, multiset flat - and fuzzy falls by more than a point, the real gain is alignment luck and not convergence. This is the mirror image of the standing record's own retained keep, where real improved AND the multiset improved 11t -> 7t and only fuzzy dipped, by a quarter of a point; that asymmetry is the discriminant, and 'real improved so keep it' is not.
